  <section class="story-section">
    <h2>Florida’s hurricane pattern, in context</h2>

    <p>To understand how Florida’s hurricanes have changed, I visited NOAA’s official hurricane database, often called <a href="https://www.aoml.noaa.gov/hrd/hurdat/Data_Storm.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HURDAT2</a>, and a summary of <a href="https://www.aoml.noaa.gov/hrd/hurdat/All_U.S._Hurricanes.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">“Continental United States Hurricane Impacts/Landfalls”</a> that lists which storms struck each state. These records track storm names, dates, paths, maximum sustained winds, and <a href="https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/aboutsshws.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Saffir–Simpson category</a> at landfall, which allows for a clean comparison of how many hurricanes hit Florida each year and how strong they were when they did.</p>

    <p>Looking across the last twenty‑plus years, the pattern is uneven, but telling. Florida has seen very active periods — such as the mid‑2000s, when multiple hurricanes made landfall in back‑to‑back seasons — and calmer stretches with fewer direct hits but still damaging storms. The big picture from recent climate research, though, is that while the number of tropical cyclones varies quite a bit from year to year, a larger share of the storms that do form are now reaching major hurricane strength (Category 3 or higher). In other words, intensity is trending up, even when storm counts bounce around.</p>

    <p>For Floridians, that matters far more than basic global averages. On the ground, higher‑category landfalls look like stronger winds, denser storm surges, and a bigger gap between what homes and infrastructure were designed to handle and what the storm actually delivers. Dr. Jennifer Collins, a professor at the University of South Florida’s School of Geosciences whose research focuses on extreme weather, said [fourth person contacted, response pending].</p>
